  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how several Standard Model (SM) processes can be mimicked by new-physics (NP) mechanisms, shown side by side in blue and pink columns. Overall, it illustrates a recurring theme: familiar rare or loop-suppressed reactions in the SM can instead arise through tree-level exchange of hypothetical particles such as leptoquarks, extra neutral gauge bosons, heavy scalars, or diquarks.\n\nOn the left, the SM examples involve conventional loop diagrams or known boson exchange, including flavor-changing radiative and semileptonic transitions, meson mixing, Higgs production and decay, and dilepton production through electroweak bosons. On the right, each is paired with a simpler-looking NP diagram where a single new mediator directly connects quark and lepton lines or alters gauge-boson and Higgs interactions.\n\nThe visible labels emphasize this contrast: loops with top quarks or electroweak bosons in the SM versus “LQ,” “Z′,” heavy scalar states, and “DQ” in NP. Several rows focus on flavor physics, showing how decays involving muons, electrons, taus, neutrinos, or quark flavor changes could be modified. The Higgs-related panels similarly compare gluon-fusion and diboson signatures in the SM with alternative heavy-particle explanations. Overall, the figure is a compact map of experimental channels sensitive to beyond-the-Standard-Model effects.",
